# Caddyfile — front the GLA gateway with Caddy: terminate public TLS and reverse-proxy to GLA on :3000.
#
# This is a TEMPLATE (the lower-trust payload tier): the executing agent fills the ⟨…⟩ placeholders for THIS
# host, then either drops it in as the host's Caddyfile or merges this site block into an existing one
# (reload, don't replace — see the edge-proxy setup task). The receipt records the checksum of what was
# actually written and the inverse op (remove this block / restore the prior Caddyfile, reload Caddy).
#
# What it does (docs/01 §2 "Access Gateway", docs/03 §3): Caddy is the SOLE public entry. It serves the
# operator's public URL over TLS and proxies every request — including the WebSocket upgrade the live-view
# session and the gateway's verified upgrades depend on — to the address that reaches GLA's gateway on :3000.
# GLA's own GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL (gla-core's env) MUST equal the public URL served here, because handoff and
# enrollment links are built against it and must resolve, through this proxy, back to the same daemon.
#
# Supported public-base shapes:
#   • dedicated root:  GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://gla.example/
#   • subpath mount:   GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://gla.example/team-a/
# The Access Gateway remains the authorization membrane in both cases. Caddy terminates TLS and routes bytes
# (including WebSocket upgrades); GLA still verifies grants, recipients, auth strength, and mounted route scope.
#
# ── The upstream: point at whatever actually reaches GLA's :3000 on THIS topology ──────────────────────────
#   • Caddy and GLA on the SAME host (or same container)        → reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
#   • Caddy on the HOST, GLA in a CONTAINER (the hermes-1 case) → reverse_proxy to the host-side forward, NOT
#     the container address. hermes-1 runs an LXD forkproxy mapping host:13000 → container:3000, so the
#     upstream is 127.0.0.1:13000. Set ⟨gla-upstream⟩ to that forward's host:port.
#
# ── TLS: Caddy auto-provisions a Let's Encrypt cert for a real domain. For a bare-IP deploy (no domain), an
#    automatic public cert is not issuable — use Caddy's internal CA or a provided cert, and accept that
#    WebAuthn/passkeys need a trusted https origin (see the identity-provider bundle). ──────────────────────

# The public site address Caddy serves — the host in GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL.
#   domain example:   gla.example.com
#   hermes-1 (IP):    203.0.113.10        (bare IP → pair with `tls internal` or a supplied cert below)
⟨gla.example.com⟩ {
	# Reverse-proxy everything to GLA's gateway. Caddy v2 proxies WebSocket upgrades transparently — the
	# Upgrade/Connection handshake is forwarded as-is, so the live-view (noVNC) and verified-upgrade paths work.
	reverse_proxy ⟨127.0.0.1:13000⟩

	# Bare-IP / no public domain: uncomment to let Caddy serve its own (locally-trusted) certificate instead
	# of attempting a public ACME cert it cannot get for an IP literal. For a real domain, delete this line and
	# Caddy obtains a Let's Encrypt cert automatically.
	# tls internal

	# Optional: keep the access log so a failed handoff can be traced at the edge.
	log
}

# ── Subpath / custom-base variant ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Use this shape when GLA is mounted under an existing site prefix (example: https://gla.example/team-a/).
# Set gla-core's env to:
#   GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://gla.example/team-a/
#
# `handle_path` strips /team-a before proxying to GLA's root-shaped internal routes (/enroll,
# /handoff/auth/*, /handoff/<id>). `X-Forwarded-Prefix` records the stripped public prefix so GLA can verify
# the request belongs to the configured public base. Because this header is trusted by GLA only when explicitly
# enabled, gla-core must also set GLA_TRUST_FORWARDED_PREFIX=true and this edge must overwrite/sanitize any
# client-supplied X-Forwarded-Prefix before proxying. WebSocket upgrades through /team-a/handoff/<id> are still
# proxied by Caddy and authorized by GLA, not by Caddy.
#
# ⟨gla.example.com⟩ {
# 	handle_path /team-a/* {
# 		header_up X-Forwarded-Prefix /team-a
# 		reverse_proxy ⟨127.0.0.1:13000⟩
# 	}
# 	log
# }
#
# Existing/custom reverse proxies that preserve the prefix instead of stripping it are also supported by GLA
# when GLA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL contains that same prefix; they should forward /team-a/enroll and /team-a/handoff/*
# unchanged to the gateway and do not need GLA_TRUST_FORWARDED_PREFIX.
